{"name":"Nightshade Berries","hex":"#1b1811","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nightshade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the poisonous plants belonging to the genus Solanum, especially black nightshade or woody nightshade.","Any plant of the wider Solanaceae family, including the nightshades as well as tomato, potato, eggplant, and deadly nightshade."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nightshade"},{"term":"berries","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of berry"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of berry"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berries"}],"entries":[{"title":"Solanaceae","blocks":[{"text":"Solanaceae, commonly known as the nightshades, is a family of flowering plants in the order Solanales. The family contains approximately 2,700 species, several of which are used as agricultural crops, medicinal plants, and ornamental plants. Many members of the family have high alkaloid contents, making some highly toxic, but many—such as tomatoes, potatoes, eggplants and peppers—are commonly used in food around the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Brugmansia_lg.jpg/330px-Brugmansia_lg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solanaceae","wikidata":"Q134172"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berbe